WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:02.234
-Welcome back.

00:00:02.234 --> 00:00:03.567
-Thank you so much, Jimmy.

00:00:03.567 --> 00:00:05.100
-Kareem, it's always
good to see you.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.501
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:00:06.501 --> 00:00:07.934
You look great.
-I'm trying.

00:00:07.934 --> 00:00:10.901
-You were -- You were born and
raised in New York City.

00:00:10.901 --> 00:00:12.133
-Yes.
-Yeah.

00:00:12.133 --> 00:00:14.367
And I was doing
a little research

00:00:14.367 --> 00:00:17.334
about your earlier days.
And I read somewhere

00:00:17.334 --> 00:00:18.734
that you were friends
with Billy Crystal.

00:00:18.734 --> 00:00:20.534
-Yes.
-How did you guys

00:00:20.534 --> 00:00:22.167
become friends?
-Well, you know,

00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:25.000
I went to high school here in
Manhattan at Power Memorial.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.868
And one of Billy's best friends
went to -- was my classmate.

00:00:28.868 --> 00:00:31.067
And he always wanted me
to come out

00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:33.000
and hang out in Long Beach,
Long Island.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.367
-Yeah.
-It's just...

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:36.267
[ Crowd cheers ]
It's too far for me.

00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:37.801
I lived at
the other end of Manhattan.

00:00:37.801 --> 00:00:39.534
[ Laughter ]

00:00:39.534 --> 00:00:41.767
Finally, before we graduated,
I went out there

00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:46.300
to visit Neil,
our mutual friend.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.601
So, we went over
to Billy's house.

00:00:48.601 --> 00:00:52.534
And his mother answers the door
and says, "Billy's not here.

00:00:52.534 --> 00:00:54.901
You guys better come back
in an hour or two.

00:00:54.901 --> 00:00:57.701
He's over with his friends,
doing something."

00:00:57.701 --> 00:00:59.067
And we couldn't find him.

00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:02.501
And - But the funniest thing
about that was,

00:01:02.501 --> 00:01:06.501
Billy's mom, from that point on,
said, "Oh, I've known --

00:01:06.501 --> 00:01:08.367
I've known Kareem
longer than Billy has."

00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:10.934
[ Laughter ]

00:01:10.934 --> 00:01:14.234
-Technically, yeah, she's right.
She technically knows --

00:01:14.234 --> 00:01:17.601
Yeah, she's right.
Was he -- Was he funny?

00:01:17.601 --> 00:01:19.400
-He was always funny.
-Yeah.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:20.734
Did you play basketball
with him?

00:01:20.734 --> 00:01:23.634
-No, but we played
a game of badminton.

00:01:23.634 --> 00:01:25.634
[ Laughter ]

00:01:25.634 --> 00:01:27.868
It's kind of tough on him
'cause all I had to do was

00:01:27.868 --> 00:01:30.667
stand at the net and
hold my racquet up like that.

00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:33.000
[ Laughter ]
-Whssh! Yeah, yeah.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.067
Maybe we'll get a rematch
one day.

00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:36.634
-Maybe, maybe.

00:01:36.634 --> 00:01:38.567
-The NBA playoffs wrapped up
a few nights ago.

00:01:38.567 --> 00:01:41.868
Golden State Warriors
beat the Boston Celtics...

00:01:41.868 --> 00:01:43.367
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yeah.

00:01:43.367 --> 00:01:44.734
-...to become the NBA champions.

00:01:44.734 --> 00:01:47.234
Steph Curry
was just making 3-pointers.

00:01:47.234 --> 00:01:49.334
That guy is just unbelievable.
Never seen anything like that.

00:01:49.334 --> 00:01:50.868
When you were playing,
were there people

00:01:50.868 --> 00:01:52.834
that shot like that?
-Not like that.

00:01:52.834 --> 00:01:54.767
But there were
good 3-point shooters,

00:01:54.767 --> 00:01:56.334
we thought, at that time.

00:01:56.334 --> 00:01:58.200
But earlier on,

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:03.000
you might get 10
3-point shots in a game.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.567
Now there's 10, 20
3-point shots in a quarter.

00:02:07.567 --> 00:02:08.968
-Yeah, exactly.

00:02:08.968 --> 00:02:12.567
-It seems like it's
a 3-point shooting contest.

00:02:12.567 --> 00:02:17.000
People enjoy it,
and you know, they should.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.200
I -- I was the -- I am, still,

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.567
the all-time leading scorer
in the NBA.

00:02:23.567 --> 00:02:25.934
And I only made
one 3-point shot.

00:02:25.934 --> 00:02:27.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:27.434 --> 00:02:28.767
-Wow.

00:02:28.767 --> 00:02:30.667
I mean, That's not --

00:02:30.667 --> 00:02:33.801
Just 'cause that was not --

00:02:33.801 --> 00:02:35.467
that's not your thing.

00:02:35.467 --> 00:02:36.868
-No, but the coach says, "Look,

00:02:36.868 --> 00:02:38.868
you shoot the ball from in here.

00:02:38.868 --> 00:02:42.367
If you come out here and shoot,
you can come sit down with me."

00:02:42.367 --> 00:02:44.200
[ Laughter ]

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:47.334
-But you only shot one
3-point shot.

00:02:47.334 --> 00:02:48.634
-Just one.
-One.

00:02:48.634 --> 00:02:50.601
-1986.
-Yes.

00:02:50.601 --> 00:02:52.000
[ Laughter ]
-And it was an accident.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.434
You know, I ran out
to the corner

00:02:53.434 --> 00:02:56.367
to get a rebound, and
I was out there all alone.

00:02:56.367 --> 00:02:57.534
They didn't want to
come guard me.

00:02:57.534 --> 00:02:59.734
I turned around and
made the 3-pointer.

00:02:59.734 --> 00:03:02.167
-We actually have found
the footage.

00:03:02.167 --> 00:03:06.167
Here's Kareem Abdul-Jabbar's
first and only 3-point shot

00:03:06.167 --> 00:03:08.267
of his career.
Take a look.

00:03:08.267 --> 00:03:10.534
-Cooper misses from 18.
Kareem hustles.

00:03:10.534 --> 00:03:12.300
Gets it.
-Shoot a 3-pointer.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.834
-He's going to do it.
-3!

00:03:14.834 --> 00:03:16.534
That's the first one
of his life.

00:03:16.534 --> 00:03:18.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:18.501 --> 00:03:21.367
-Come on, bud.
[ Laughs ]

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:24.200
That's how you do it.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:26.901
-2,099.
-Yeah.

00:03:26.901 --> 00:03:29.801
-And I'm gonna be
catching Steph Curry.

00:03:29.801 --> 00:03:31.767
[ Laughter ]

00:03:31.767 --> 00:03:33.067
-You're really close
to Steph Curry.

00:03:33.067 --> 00:03:34.567
Yeah, you better watch out,
Steph.

00:03:34.567 --> 00:03:36.000
He's real close.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.033
The NBA has named an award
after you.

00:03:38.033 --> 00:03:42.467
It is the Kareem Abdul-Jabbar
Social Justice Champion Award.

00:03:42.467 --> 00:03:43.834
-Yeah.
-And every year,

00:03:43.834 --> 00:03:46.801
the award goes to a player
that helps to drive equality

00:03:46.801 --> 00:03:49.868
for historically
marginalized communities.

00:03:49.868 --> 00:03:54.000
You've said this before --
You said, "I can do more

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.400
than stuff a ball
through a hoop.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.400
The greatest asset is my mind."

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:00.834
And what does that mean
to the next generation

00:04:00.834 --> 00:04:03.133
following your lead,
off the court?

00:04:03.133 --> 00:04:05.634
-I think the NBA
has done a great job

00:04:05.634 --> 00:04:10.133
of encouraging the players
to do what they can

00:04:10.133 --> 00:04:12.334
to help the communities
where they play.

00:04:12.334 --> 00:04:14.667
You know, we provide
a lot of entertainment.

00:04:14.667 --> 00:04:17.167
But people need real help.

00:04:17.167 --> 00:04:23.534
So, the fact that they've
named this award after me

00:04:23.534 --> 00:04:25.634
is very gratifying to me.

00:04:25.634 --> 00:04:29.434
But what it does
for the communities

00:04:29.434 --> 00:04:32.267
that these guys try to help --
I mean, that --

00:04:32.267 --> 00:04:34.601
those are the things
that lift us all, and...

00:04:34.601 --> 00:04:36.934
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:36.934 --> 00:04:40.100
-It's amazing what you've done.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:42.701
And we've talked about it.
You've been on the show before.

00:04:42.701 --> 00:04:45.367
But Juneteenth was yesterday.

00:04:45.367 --> 00:04:48.367
And when it became a
federal holiday, last year,

00:04:48.367 --> 00:04:52.033
you said that you were
more relieved than happy.

00:04:52.033 --> 00:04:53.767
Can you explain that?

00:04:53.767 --> 00:04:56.000
-Well, I think
it's been too long.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:58.634
You know, it took us --
It took too long

00:04:58.634 --> 00:05:02.267
before people started thinking
about celebrating that

00:05:02.267 --> 00:05:03.868
nationwide.
-Yeah.

00:05:03.868 --> 00:05:05.300
-It was only
in the black community,

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:08.634
where it's been celebrated
for many years.

00:05:08.634 --> 00:05:14.133
But I think that the things
that make America great,

00:05:14.133 --> 00:05:16.734
we're always anxious
to talk about it.

00:05:16.734 --> 00:05:19.534
But we can't talk
about the things that are tough

00:05:19.534 --> 00:05:20.968
here in America.

00:05:20.968 --> 00:05:23.667
And if we don't talk about them,
we can't ever change them.

00:05:23.667 --> 00:05:26.100
So, as long as we can
get to the point

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.501
that we can talk about
these things,

00:05:28.501 --> 00:05:30.200
we can make positive change.

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:32.300
And that's what it should be
all about.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:33.667
-Yeah, good. I'm glad.

00:05:33.667 --> 00:05:36.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:36.501 --> 00:05:40.968
On top of everything you do,

00:05:40.968 --> 00:05:42.901
you're also
an award-winning writer.

00:05:42.901 --> 00:05:46.334
We've talked about this
on the show when you come on.

00:05:46.334 --> 00:05:48.634
You get writing awards --

00:05:48.634 --> 00:05:50.801
You actually have
more writing awards

00:05:50.801 --> 00:05:52.567
than you have NBA championships.

00:05:52.567 --> 00:05:54.901
Last year,
you started a Substack.

00:05:54.901 --> 00:05:56.267
-Yeah.
-You write about everything.

00:05:56.267 --> 00:05:58.334
You write about
political issues.

00:05:58.334 --> 00:06:01.501
You reviewed "Top Gun,"
I thought was interesting.

00:06:01.501 --> 00:06:04.767
What's it like
being your editor in chief?

00:06:04.767 --> 00:06:07.601
-Well, you know, it's always --
It's tough

00:06:07.601 --> 00:06:12.601
when your ideas and your
editor's ideas don't mesh.

00:06:12.601 --> 00:06:16.067
That's difficult.
But I don't have that problem.

00:06:16.067 --> 00:06:20.200
And you know, I can be Kareem
the sports commentator,

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:22.601
Kareem
the political commentator.

00:06:22.601 --> 00:06:25.434
I can be Kareem
the ordinary journalist.

00:06:25.434 --> 00:06:27.968
-Restaurant reviewer.
-Yeah. You name it.

00:06:27.968 --> 00:06:30.434
And I'm always
down for a free meal.

00:06:30.434 --> 00:06:31.834
[ Laughter ]

00:06:31.834 --> 00:06:34.200
-Me too, me too.
Count me in, count me in.

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:36.100
[ Applause ]

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:38.901
I want to mention your charity,
the skyhook foundation.

00:06:38.901 --> 00:06:41.734
I love this.

00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:44.901
I love what you do.
Especially I love helping kids.

00:06:44.901 --> 00:06:47.400
And you're doing
something very, very cool

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:50.033
with this artist named Charlie
Palmer.

00:06:50.033 --> 00:06:52.467
-Charlie Palmer, yeah.
-He created this portrait

00:06:52.467 --> 00:06:54.434
of you.
Look at this.

00:06:54.434 --> 00:06:57.067
Yeah. Amazing.

00:06:57.067 --> 00:07:01.033
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:01.033 --> 00:07:03.133
There are 500 of these?

00:07:03.133 --> 00:07:05.834
-There's 500,
and we're selling them.

00:07:05.834 --> 00:07:08.300
You can go to
kareemabduljabbar.com

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:09.701
if you want to buy one.

00:07:09.701 --> 00:07:12.501
And the proceeds go
to the Skyhook Foundation

00:07:12.501 --> 00:07:16.934
to help keep us above water
so that we don't drown.

00:07:16.934 --> 00:07:20.701
We take kids out
into the forest,

00:07:20.701 --> 00:07:23.267
the Angeles National Forest.

00:07:23.267 --> 00:07:28.801
and they do S.T.E.M. and
S.T.E.A.M. programs.

00:07:28.801 --> 00:07:30.601
-Learn how to program,
do everything.

00:07:30.601 --> 00:07:32.534
But also learn
how to look at a tree.

00:07:32.534 --> 00:07:35.901
-How to look at a tree,
water quality, soil quality,

00:07:35.901 --> 00:07:37.868
flora and fauna.

00:07:37.868 --> 00:07:42.133
And it helps the kids learn
that the world is bigger

00:07:42.133 --> 00:07:45.167
than their little neighborhood
in L.A.

00:07:45.167 --> 00:07:47.534
A lot of them haven't ever been
out of their neighborhood.

00:07:47.534 --> 00:07:50.434
And they can see
the Angeles National Forest

00:07:50.434 --> 00:07:52.200
from their neighborhood,
but they've never been there.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:54.667
So, you know, we're
expanding their world

00:07:54.667 --> 00:07:58.434
and making it possible for them
to find out, you know,

00:07:58.434 --> 00:08:01.701
what's out there in the world
that they can hook onto

00:08:01.701 --> 00:08:04.367
that will give them
a meaningful life.

00:08:04.367 --> 00:08:06.634
-I love that, Kareem.
Thank you for doing that.

00:08:06.634 --> 00:08:08.467
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:08:08.467 --> 00:08:11.334
For more information
on the Skyhook Foundation

00:08:11.334 --> 00:08:14.767
and Kareem's Substack
visit kareemabduljabbar.com.

00:08:14.767 --> 00:08:16.634
Kareem and I are doing
something fun after the break.

00:08:16.634 --> 00:08:18.300
Stick around. Come on back.
-Come on back.

